docs/schwab-api-contract.md:44:| Token response | `{"expires_in":1800,"token_type":"Bearer","scope":"api","refresh_token","access_token","id_token"}`; `id_token` is not persisted |
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:45:| Access token | 30 minutes; expired tokens are rejected outright (API 401) |
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:46:| Refresh token | 7 days from the authorization-code grant. **Refreshing does not extend it.** `refresh_expires_at` is set once at issuance and never moved |
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:47:| Refresh response | includes a `refresh_token`; store whatever comes back, keep `refresh_expires_at` |
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:125:`startDate`/`endDate` are epoch ms and override `period`. Valid combinations: day (1,2,3,4,5,10) with minute (1,5,10,15,30); month (1,2,3,6) with daily or weekly; year (1,2,3,5,10,15,20) with daily, weekly, monthly; ytd (1) with daily or weekly. Response `{"candles":[{"open","high","low","close","volume","datetime":<epoch ms>}],"symbol","empty","previousClose","previousCloseDate"}`. Daily `datetime` is midnight Eastern in UTC ms: convert in America/New_York. For the collar engine: `periodType=month&period=3&frequencyType=daily`.
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:144:`Schwab::FakeClient` sits **below the mappers**: it returns scrubbed raw hashes in the same shape as the HTTP client, so seeds and Playwright exercise the mapping code where every shape assumption lives, and its fixtures double as the cassette-shaped fixtures for specs. It scripts auth too (`authorize_url`, `exchange_code`, `refresh`) and supports a per-call response queue so a test can say "first `orders` call raises RateLimited, second succeeds". Scenarios: re-auth success and failure, refresh expired, rate limited then success, server error then success, transport timeout, empty account, position removed between syncs, nested bracket child, transaction with missing CUSIP, unknown enum value, closed market day, quotes batch with one invalid symbol, and the four dashboard banner states.
